  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows an impressive fetch of moisture stretching \nthrough Mexico, up through the the mid Mississippi River Valley, and \nthen up trough New England. This moisture axis is oriented roughly \nparallel to a surface cold frontal boundary and along the mid and \nupper jet axis over our area. Given good upper level divergence and \na responding low level jet beneath it, there is plenty of lift \nthrough the atmospheric column combined with deep moisture pushing \nPWATs over 2\". The end result will be widespread rainfall today with \nperhaps a few pockets of embedded thunderstorms. Overall mid level \nlapse rates are expected to remain poor today combined with meager \nML CAPE values likely 500 J/KG or less. Ultimately, heavy cloud \ncover and a steady NW/N wind behind the frontal passage will limit \nthe potential to destabilize, and the potential for any stronger \nstorms.\n\nOverall rainfall amounts are expected to mostly be in the .5\" to 1\" \nrange across the area, although a few pockets of amounts around 1.5\" \nare possible especially along and north of I-64 where the most \nresidence time is expected. There will be quite a temperature \ngradient from NW to SE across the area today with areas north of the \ncold frontal boundary only reaching the low and mid 70s. SE of the \nboundary, look for highs in the low and mid 80s. This line should \nstretch from roughly Bowling Green, KY up through Winchester, KY.\n\nThe surface boundary stalls across our far SE CWA later tonight into \nSaturday with a few lingering showers along the KY/TN border and \nthrough the Lake Cumberland region. These chances will basically \nlinger until a shortwave trough axis finally pushes through later \nSaturday into Sunday morning. Outside of the scattered shower \nchances down south through the first part of the weekend, the rest \nof the CWA will be dry as Canadian surface high pressure gets more \nestablished over southern Indiana and north central KY. Looking for \nhighs on Saturday in the upper 60s and low 70s across the area under \na steady NE surface wind. Overnight lows will be chilly in the upper \n40s and 50s across the area.\n\nSunday, we sit in the right entrance region of the UL jet streak. \nThis should provide decent lift; combined with our lingering surface \ncold front, expect some lingering sprinkles to showers in the Lake \nCumberland area. Post-frontal highs should be around the 75 degree \nmark, with Bowling Green and the southern part of the region perhaps \npushing 80 if cloud cover clears quickly enough to allow surface \nheating. Showers should fully leave the area by Monday morning, and \novernight lows are in the upper 40s and low 50s. Southern Indiana \nand Ohio Valley should clear out clouds by Sunday afternoon while \nthe Bowling Green/Lake Cumberland takes until at least Monday \nmorning to reach fully clear skies with dry air at 850mb. Monday \nshould bring highs around 70 for S IN/Ohio Valley and 75 for S KY. \nShould be breezy with sustained northeasterly winds around 10 mph \nand gusts up to 18 mph possible. Overnight lows should be in the low \nto mid 40s. Frost is doubtful for this week, but probably not far \naway.\n\nTuesday through Thursday, the surface High swings across the Great \nLakes, stalling out the cold sfc front south of our CWA - this \nshould lead to light easterly/northeasterly flow at the surface. The \nUL ridge over the Great Plains should weaken enough to lead to solid \nW to E flow aloft. We mix a bit of moisture back in, leading us to \npush up to highs in he upper 70s and potentially breaking 80 on \nThursday and Friday for Bowling Green and the Louisville metro. Lows \nshould remain in the upper 40s and low 50s.",
